"GitHub - AmamiSora1228/MMLongEmbed · GitHub (https://github.com/AmamiSora1228/MMLongEmbed)\nciteturn43727view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/AmamiSora1228/MMLongEmbed\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 401\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL15:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL16:       * 【6†CodespacesInstant dev environments】\nL17:       * 【7†IssuesPlan and track work】\nL18:       * 【8†Code ReviewManage code changes】\nL19:       * 【9†Code QualityEnforce quality at merge】\nL20:     * APPLICATION SECURITY\nL21:       * 【10†GitHub Advanced SecurityFind and fix vulnerabilities】\nL22:       * 【11†Code securitySecure your code as you build】\nL23:       * 【12†Secret protectionStop leaks before they start】\nL24: \nL25:     * EXPLORE\nL26:       * 【13†Why GitHub】\nL27:       * 【14†Documentation†docs.github.com】\nL28:       * 【15†Blog†github.blog】\nL29:       * 【16†Changelog†github.blog】\nL30:       * 【17†Marketplace】\nL31: \nL32: 【18†View all features】\nL33: \nL34:   * Solutions\nL35:     * BY COMPANY SIZE\nL36:       * 【19†Enterprises】\nL37:       * 【20†Small and medium teams】\nL38:       * 【21†Startups】\nL39:       * 【22†Nonprofits】\nL40: \nL41:     * BY USE CASE\nL42:       * 【23†App Modernization】\nL43:       * 【24†DevSecOps】\nL44:       * 【25†DevOps】\nL45:       * 【26†CI/CD】\nL46:       * 【27†View all use cases】\nL47: \nL48:     * BY INDUSTRY\nL49:       * 【28†Healthcare】\nL50:       * 【29†Financial services】\nL51:       * 【30†Manufacturing】\nL52:       * 【31†Government】\nL53:       * 【32†See all industries】\nL54: \nL55: 【33†View all solutions】\nL56: \nL57:   * Resources\nL58:     * EXPLORE BY TOPIC\nL59:       * 【34†AI】\nL60:       * 【35†Software Development】\nL61:       * 【36†DevOps】\nL62:       * 【37†Security】\nL63:       * 【38†View all topics】\nL64: \nL65:     * EXPLORE BY TYPE\nL66:       * 【39†Customer stories】\nL67:       * 【40†Events & webinars】\nL68:       * 【41†Ebooks & reports】\nL69:       * 【42†Business insights】\nL70:       * 【43†GitHub Skills†skills.github.com】\nL71:     * SUPPORT & SERVICES\nL72:       * 【14†Documentation†docs.github.com】\nL73:       * 【44†Customer support†support.github.com】\nL74:       * 【45†Community forum】\nL75:       * 【46†Trust center】\nL76:       * 【47†Partners】\nL77: \nL78: 【48†View all resources】\nL79: \nL80:   * Open Source\nL81: \nL82:     * COMMUNITY\nL83:       * 【49†GitHub SponsorsFund open source developers】\nL84:     * PROGRAMS\nL85:       * 【50†Security Lab†securitylab.github.com】\nL86:       * 【51†Maintainer Community†maintainers.github.com】\nL87:       * 【52†GitHub Stars†stars.github.com】\nL88:       * 【53†Archive Program†archiveprogram.github.com】\nL89: \nL90:     * REPOSITORIES\nL91:       * 【54†Topics】\nL92:       * 【55†Trending】\nL93:       * 【56†Collections】\nL94: \nL95:   * Enterprise\nL96: \nL97:     * ENTERPRISE SOLUTIONS\nL98:       * 【19†Enterprise platformAI-powered developer platform】\nL99:     * AVAILABLE ADD-ONS\nL100:       * 【10†GitHub Advanced SecurityEnterprise-grade security features】\nL101:       * 【57†Copilot for BusinessEnterprise-grade AI features】\nL102:       * 【58†Premium SupportEnterprise-grade 24/7 support】\nL103: \nL104:   * 【59†Pricing】\nL105: \nL106: Search`/`\nL107: \nL108: 【1†Sign in】\nL109: \nL110: 【60†Sign up】\nL111: \nL112: Appearance settings\nL113: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL114: \nL115:   1. 【61†AmamiSora1228】\nL116:   2. 【62†MMLongEmbed】\nL117: ## Repository navigation\nL118: \nL119:   *   * 【62†Code】\nL120:   * 【63†Issues】\nL121:   * 【64†Pull requests】\nL122:   * 【65†Actions】\nL123:   * 【66†Projects】\nL124:   * 【67†Security and quality】\nL125:   * 【68†Insights】\nL126: \nL127: More items\nL128: \nL129: 【69†Image: AmamiSora1228†avatars.githubusercontent.com】\nL130: \nL131: 【62†MMLongEmbed】\nL132: \nL133: Public\nL134: \nL135:   * 【70†Notifications】You must be signed in to change notification settings\nL136:   * 【70†Fork0 (0)】\nL137:   * 【70†Star2 (2)】You must be signed in to star a repository\nL138: \nL139: ## About\nL140: \nL141: No description, website, or topics provided.\nL142: ### Resources\nL143: \nL144: 【71†Readme】\nL145: \nL146: 【72†Activity】\nL147: \nL148: ### Stars\nL149: \nL150: 2 stars\nL151: \nL152: ### Watchers\nL153: \nL154: 0 watching\nL155: \nL156: ### Forks\nL157: \nL158: 【73†0 forks】\nL159: \nL160: 【74†Report repository】\nL161: \nL162: main\nL163: \nL164: 【75†Branches】【76†Tags】\nL165: \nL166: [Input: Go to file]\nL167: \nL168: Go to file\nL169: \nL170: Code\nL171: \nL172: Open more actions menu\nL173: \nL174: ## Latest commit\nL175: \nL176: \nL177: \nL178: ## History\nL179: \nL180: 17 Commits\nL181: ## Folders and files\nL182: \nL183: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL184: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL185: 【77†configs】  | 【77†configs】  |    |\nL186: 【78†mmlongembed】  | 【78†mmlongembed】  |    |\nL187: 【79†scripts】  | 【79†scripts】  |    |\nL188: 【80†.gitignore】  | 【80†.gitignore】  |    |\nL189: 【81†README.md】  | 【81†README.md】  |    |\nL190: 【82†pyproject.toml】  | 【82†pyproject.toml】  |    |\nL191: \nL192: ## Repository files navigation\nL193: \nL194:   *   * 【83†README】\nL195: \nL196: More items\nL197: # MMLongEmbed\nL198: \nL199: Multimodal Long-Context Embedding Evaluation Framework\nL200: \nL201: MMLongEmbed is a benchmarking framework for evaluating multimodal long-context embedding models. It supports context lengths up to 32k tokens across text, image, and video modalities, covering 8+ tasks in 4 categories with 11+ model backbones.\nL202: ## Task Categories\nL203: \nL204: Category  | Tasks  | Description\nL205: --- | --- | ---\nL206: Visual Grounding  | NeedleBench, MM-NIAH  | Locate relevant visual information in long contexts\nL207: Multi-Source Reasoning  | MMDocRAG, HotpotQA  | Synthesize answers from multiple documents/images\nL208: Logical Summarization  | LongBench, MMLongBench  | Condense long multimodal documents\nL209: Temporal Summarization  | YoukuDC, ActivityNetCaptions  | Summarize video content over time\nL210: Each task is evaluated at multiple context lengths: 1k, 2k, 4k, 8k, 16k, 32k tokens.\nL211: ## Supported Models\nL212: \nL213: Model  | Backend Key\nL214: --- | ---\nL215: Qwen3-VL-Embedding (2B/8B)  | `qwen3_vl`\nL216: GME-Qwen2-VL-Instruct (2B/7B)  | `gme_qwen2_vl_instruct`\nL217: E5-Omni (7B)  | `e5_omni`\nL218: Jina Embeddings V4  | `jina_embeddings_v4`\nL219: MM-Embed  | `mm_embed`\nL220: RzenEmbed  | `rzen_embed`\nL221: VLM2Vec V2  | `vlm2vec_v2`\nL222: Ume-R1  | `ume_r1`\nL223: Embed-RL  | `embed_rl`\nL224: ## Installation\nL225: \nL226:     # Clone and enter the repository\nL227:     git clone <repo-url>\nL228:     cd MMLongEmbed\nL229: \nL230:     # Create conda environment (recommended)\nL231:     conda create -n mmlongembed python=3.11 -y\nL232:     conda activate mmlongembed\nL233: \nL234:     # Install PyTorch (see https://pytorch.org for your CUDA version)\nL235:     pip install torch>=2.5.0 torchvision\nL236: \nL237:     # Install MMLongEmbed and dependencies\nL238:     pip install -e .\nL239:     pip install flash-attn --no-build-isolation\nL240: ### Key Dependencies\nL241: \nL242:   * Python >= 3.10\nL243:   * PyTorch >= 2.5.0\nL244:   * transformers >= 4.45.0\nL245:   * flash-attn (for efficient attention)\nL246:   * See `pyproject.toml` for full list\nL247: \nL248: ## Quick Start\nL249: ### 1. Prepare Data\nL250: Place datasets under `data/instruction/` with the following structure:\nL251: \nL252:     `data/\nL253:     ├── instruction/\nL254:     │   ├── log_summar/        # Logical summarization tasks\nL255:     │   │   ├── mmlongbench/\nL256:     │   │   └── gov_report/\nL257:     │   ├── mul_reason/        # Multi-source reasoning\nL258:     │   │   ├── mmdocrag/\nL259:     │   │   └── hotpotqa/\nL260:     │   ├── vis_ground/        # Visual grounding\nL261:     │   │   ├── mminah/\nL262:     │   │   └── needlebench/\nL263:     │   └── tem_summar/        # Temporal summarization\nL264:     │       ├── activitynet/\nL265:     │       └── youkudc/\nL266:     ├── images/                # Image assets\nL267:     └── video/                 # Video frame assets (tem_summar)\nL268:     `\nL269: Each task directory contains JSONL files with query-candidate pairs. See `configs/` for task-specific YAML configurations.\nL270: ### 2. Run Evaluation\nL271: \nL272: Single model on a single task:\nL273: \nL274:     python -m mmlongembed.eval \\\nL275:         --model_name_or_path Qwen/Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B \\\nL276:         --model_backbone qwen3_vl \\\nL277:         --gpu_ids 0,1,2,3 \\\nL278:         --dataset_config configs/log_summar/gov_report.yaml \\\nL279:         --data_basedir ./data/ \\\nL280:         --output_dir outputs/\nL281: > Note: `--data_basedir ./data/` points to the `data/` directory containing `instruction/` and `images/`/`video/` subdirectories. Each config's `anno_dir` and `image_dir` are resolved relative to this base.\nL282: ### 3. Batch Evaluation\nL283: \nL284: Edit `scripts/eval.sh` with your model list and run:\nL285: \nL286:     bash scripts/eval.sh\nL287: ### Key Arguments\nL288: Argument  | Description  | Default\nL289: --- | --- | ---\nL290: `--model_name_or_path`  | Model path or HuggingFace ID  | required\nL291: `--model_backbone`  | Backend key from supported models list  | required\nL292: `--dataset_config`  | YAML config file path  | required\nL293: `--data_basedir`  | Base directory for data (config paths resolved here)  | `./data/`\nL294: `--gpu_ids`  | Comma-separated GPU IDs  | `\"0\"`\nL295: `--dispatch_batch_size`  | Samples per GPU dispatch  | `500`\nL296: `--infer_batch_size`  | Batch size per inference step  | `1`\nL297: `--dtype`  | Torch dtype  | `bfloat16`\nL298: `--max_length`  | Max sequence length  | `32768`\nL299: `--tensor_parallel_size`  | Tensor parallelism size  | `1`\nL300: `--output_dir`  | Results output directory  | `outputs/`\nL301: ## Data Format\nL302: Datasets use JSONL format with multimodal query-candidate pairs:\nL303: \nL304:     {\nL305:       \"id\": \"sample_001\",\nL306:       \"instruction_query\": \"Find the document that matches this query.\",\nL307:       \"instruction_cand\": \"Represent this document for retrieval.\",\nL308:       \"query\": [\nL309:         {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Long text query...\"},\nL310:         {\"type\": \"image\", \"image\": \"path/to/query_img.jpg\"}\nL311:       ],\nL312:       \"candidates\": [\nL313:         [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Candidate document 1...\"}, {\"type\": \"image\", \"image\": \"path/to/img1.jpg\"}],\nL314:         [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Candidate document 2...\"}]\nL315:       ],\nL316:       \"metadata\": {\nL317:         \"candidate_ids\": [\"cand_0\", \"cand_1\"],\nL318:         \"ground_truth\": \"cand_0\",\nL319:       }\nL320:     }\nL321: ## Output Structure\nL322: \nL323:     `outputs/\nL324:     └── <model_name>/\nL325:         └── <task_category>/\nL326:             └── <dataset_parser>/\nL327:                 └── <dataset_name>/\nL328:                     ├── qry.jsonl       # Query embeddings\nL329:                     ├── cand.jsonl      # Candidate embeddings\nL330:                     ├── metrics.json    # Evaluation metrics (Hit@k, NDCG, MRR, etc.)\nL331:                     └── details.jsonl   # Per-sample prediction details\nL332:     `\nL333: ## Metrics\nL334: \nL335:   * Hit@k: Whether the correct candidate is in top-k predictions\nL336:   * NDCG: Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain\nL337:   * MRR: Mean Reciprocal Rank\nL338:   * Precision/Recall: Standard retrieval metrics\nL339: ## Project Structure\nL340: \nL341:     `MMLongEmbed/\nL342:     ├── mmlongembed/\nL343:     │   ├── eval.py                      # Main evaluation entry point\nL344:     │   └── src/\nL345:     │       ├── arguments.py             # CLI argument definitions\nL346:     │       ├── data/\nL347:     │       │   ├── manager.py           # Dataset loading and preprocessing\nL348:     │       │   ├── collator.py          # Multimodal batch collation\nL349:     │       │   └── tasks/               # Task-specific dataset classes\nL350:     │       ├── model/\nL351:     │       │   ├── manager.py           # Multi-GPU inference orchestrator\nL352:     │       │   └── backbone/            # Model-specific workers and embedders\nL353:     │       ├── draw/                    # Visualization scripts\nL354:     │       └── utils/\nL355:     │           ├── evaluator.py         # Retrieval metric computation\nL356:     │           ├── metrics.py           # Ranking metrics (NDCG, MRR, etc.)\nL357:     │           ├── decorators.py        # Timer and error-handling utilities\nL358:     │           └── plot.py              # Result plotting\nL359:     ├── configs/                         # Task configuration YAML files\nL360:     ├── data/\nL361:     │   ├── instruction/                 # Dataset annotations\nL362:     │   ├── images/                      # Image assets\nL363:     │   └── video/                       # Video frame assets\nL364:     ├── scripts/\nL365:     │   └── eval.sh                      # Batch evaluation launcher\nL366:     └── outputs/                         # Experiment results\nL367:     `\nL368: ## About\nL369: \nL370: No description, website, or topics provided.\nL371: \nL372: ### Resources\nL373: \nL374: 【71†Readme】\nL375: \nL376: 【72†Activity】\nL377: \nL378: ### Stars\nL379: \nL380: 2 stars\nL381: \nL382: ### Watchers\nL383: \nL384: 0 watching\nL385: \nL386: ### Forks\nL387: \nL388: 【73†0 forks】\nL389: \nL390: 【74†Report repository】\nL391: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJoyAI-VL-Interaction (https://joyai-vl-video-future-academy-jd.github.io/JoyAI-VL-Interaction/)\nciteturn43727view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://joyai-vl-video-future-academy-jd.github.io/JoyAI-VL-Interaction/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 559\nL0: 【0†JoyAI-VL】\nL1: \nL2:   * 【1†Capabilities】\nL3:   * 【2†Approach】\nL4:   * 【3†Evaluation】\nL5:   * 【4†Authors】\nL6:   * 【5†BibTeX】\nL7: [Button: ZH]\nL8: \nL9: Video Understanding Team of JoyAI-VL @ Joy Future Academy, JD · June 10, 2026\nL10: # JoyAI-VL-Interaction\nL11: \nL12: An Open Real-time Video-Language Interaction Model\nL13: \nL14: 【6†Report†echovideo.jd.cn】 【7†Code†github.com】 【8†Model†huggingface.co】 【9†Dataset†huggingface.co】 【1†Capabilities ↓】\nL15: \nL16: Intro video\nL17: \nL18: Overview figure\nL19: \nL20: 【10†Image: JoyAI-VL-Interaction overview】\nL21: \nL22: On this page\nL23: \nL24:   * 【11†Introduction】\nL25:   * 【1†Capabilities】\nL26:   * 【2†Our Approach】\nL27:   * 【3†Evaluation】\nL28:   * 【12†Conclusion】\nL29:   * 【4†Authors】\nL30:   * 【5†Citation】\nL31: ## Introduction\nL32: \nL33: The most important moments rarely wait for you to ask. A pot boils over while your hands are full. A toddler wanders toward the stove. The best moment of the game is gone before you can react. By the time you'd think to ask an AI, the moment has already passed, because the real world doesn't pause.\nL34: Today's AI can't help with moments like these, and it isn't a matter of speed. These models are turn-based by design: they sit quietly until you address them, then answer the question you asked. Even the video-call features in today's apps are question-and-answer underneath, reacting only when polled or asked. They were built for conversation, not for being present in a world that keeps moving.\nL35: We think the next step is a model that's present like a person: one that watches what's happening now, decides on its own when a moment is worth a word, speaks up when it matters and stays quiet when it doesn't, and hands off to a stronger model when a problem is hard. Thinking Machines Lab recently named this an interaction model. We believe it's the right direction, and we wanted to make it something anyone can build on.\nL36: So today we're releasing JoyAI-VL-Interaction: an 8B-scale, vision-first interaction model, released together with its training recipe, its data, and a complete, deployable system, all fully OPEN. Point a webcam or a livestream at it and it's immediately present in the scene, watching and responding in real time. Because the model is compact and the system runs on standard infrastructure, anyone can stand up their own always-present assistant from a single repository.\nL37: In head-to-head tests against the in-app video-call assistants of Doubao and Gemini, across 58 recorded visual-interaction cases from live commentary and monitoring to real-time memory, human raters preferred JoyAI-VL-Interaction by a wide margin on both what it says and when it says it. And along the way, the same recipe gave rise to abilities we never trained for, like guiding a shopper through changing app screens or improvising a lecture from a slide deck.\nL38: Our goal is simple: to take what interaction models make possible and put it in everyone's hands, helping move multimodal AI from turn-based dialogue toward genuine, real-time presence, openly and together. Here's what that looks like.\nL39: \nL40: 01 Real-time presence\nL41: \nL42: It stays present in a live stream for hours, watching every second and responding in under a second, instead of waking up only when you ask.\nL43: \nL44: 02 Vision-triggered proactivity\nL45: It decides for itself, from what it sees, when a moment is worth a word, speaking up the instant something matters and staying quiet when nothing does.\nL46: \nL47: 03 Agent delegation\nL48: \nL49: When a problem outgrows real-time inference, it hands the task to a background agent or API, even one that acts in the digital world, and folds the answer back in while it keeps watching.\nL50: \nL51: 04 Fully OPEN stack\nL52: We release everything, the 8B model, its training recipe, the data, and a complete deployable system, so anyone can run, reproduce, and build on it from a single repository.\nL53: ## Capabilities\nL54: Once interactivity is trained into the model itself, rather than bolted on by an external harness, a whole class of capabilities comes naturally. These are exactly the things a turn-based assistant can't do well, however fast it answers: being present, acting at the right moment, sensing time, and remembering across a long stream. Here are nine of them, each a natural advantage of building interaction into the model.\nL55: And for every demo below, we include real screen recordings of Doubao's and Gemini's video-call assistants alongside ours, so the difference in interaction style between an interaction model and a turn-based one is plain to see.\nL56: Click the buttons below to access the corresponding demos.\nL57: \nL58: 01 Real-time translation\nL59: \nL60: Translates on-screen subtitles and text in real time, keeping pace with the video as it plays rather than with a single frame.\nL61: \nL62: 02 Monitoring and alerting\nL63: \nL64: Watches a live feed and raises the alert within a second of an emergency, right when it happens.\nL65: \nL66: 03 App guidance\nL67: \nL68: Follows your phone screen as you use an app and walks you through the task in step with what's on it right now.\nL69: \nL70: 04 Live commentary\nL71: Narrates what's on screen as it unfolds, from a slide deck to a live match, keeping up in real time.\nL72: \nL73: 05 Real-time counting\nL74: \nL75: Counts target objects or actions on the fly, updating the tally each time one appears.\nL76: \nL77: 06 Time awareness\nL78: \nL79: Acts on its own sense of elapsed time, speaking on a set interval or reminding you a few seconds later.\nL80: \nL81: 07 Long visual memory (5+ min)\nL82: \nL83: Holds onto useful details from minutes earlier in the stream and brings them back when they matter.\nL84: \nL85: 08 Visual-driven interaction\nL86: Reads changes in the scene to interact more naturally, like greeting a friend the moment they step into view.\nL87: \nL88: 09 Agent delegation\nL89: \nL90: Hands hard subtasks to a background model or agent, and keeps watching and interacting while the answer comes back.\nL91: \nL92: Capability demos Demos of Capability 01 · Real-time Translation Arrows switch capabilities; sample tabs switch demos.\nL93: \nL94: Capability [Button: ‹] [Button: ›]\nL95: \nL96: Capability 01\nL97: ### Real-time Translation\nL98: \nL99: Real-time translation\nL100: \nL101: A translation assistant has to keep listening and watching as the scene changes, rather than producing one answer for the frame where the task is issued.\nL102: \nL103: Task: Help me translate subtitles in real time into Chinese/English.\nL104: \nL105: Demo 01\nL106: \nL107: #### Pharmacy Visit Animation\nL108: \nL109: Real-time translation\nL110: #### JoyAI-VL-Interaction\nL111: \nL112: Translates in real time as the video plays, catching every new subtitle the moment it changes with none left out, rather than answering only once.\nL113: \nL114: #### Doubao\nL115: \nL116: Translates only the beginning and the end, and even then slightly late, never keeping up with any of the subtitles in between in real time.\nL117: \nL118: #### Gemini\nL119: \nL120: Does not translate the subtitles, replies in English instead, and responds only once, at the moment the user asks.\nL121: \nL122: Demo 02\nL123: #### Street Interview Translation\nL124: \nL125: Real-time translation\nL126: \nL127: #### JoyAI-VL-Interaction\nL128: \nL129: Completes the task as continuous real-time translation, rendering each new subtitle the instant it appears and missing none.\nL130: \nL131: #### Doubao\nL132: \nL133: Focuses only on the subtitle in the frame at the moment the question is asked, and does not sustain translation through the later dialogue.\nL134: #### Gemini\nL135: \nL136: Focuses only on the subtitle in the frame at the moment the question is asked, and does not sustain translation through the later dialogue.\nL137: \nL138: Capability 02\nL139: \nL140: ### Monitoring and Alerting\nL141: \nL142: Monitoring and alerting\nL143: \nL144: Alerting is the clearest test of whether a model is genuinely present. The right response is neither early nor late: it should arrive when the watched event occurs.\nL145: \nL146: Task: Warn immediately when a target event or action (yellow-card event / the fall) appears.\nL147: \nL148: Demo 01\nL149: #### Yellow-card Alert\nL150: \nL151: Monitoring and alerting\nL152: \nL153: #### JoyAI-VL-Interaction\nL154: \nL155: Responds quickly when the yellow-card event appears.\nL156: \nL157: #### Doubao\nL158: \nL159: Responds roughly 20 seconds later in this case, which is too slow for a monitoring task.\nL160: \nL161: #### Gemini\nL162: \nL163: Stays focused on earlier context rather than monitoring the later visual event.\nL164: \nL165: Demo 02\nL166: \nL167: #### Fall Detection Alert\nL168: \nL169: Monitoring and alerting\nL170: \nL171: #### JoyAI-VL-Interaction\nL172: \nL173: Sends the warning immediately when the fall occurs.\nL174: #### Doubao\nL175: \nL176: Responds several seconds late, weakening its usefulness for safety monitoring.\nL177: \nL178: #### Gemini\nL179: \nL180: Treats the task like backward-looking video QA rather than a live alerting task.\nL181: \nL182: Capability 03\nL183: \nL184: ### App Guidance\nL185: \nL186: App guidance\nL187: \nL188: App guidance requires a model to follow a user's intent across changing screens, keeping the goal alive instead of describing one snapshot.\nL189: \nL190: Task: Accompany or guide the user in real time as they browse an APP.\nL191: \nL192: Demo 01\nL193: \nL194: #### Shopping App Guidance\nL195: \nL196: App guidance\nL197: #### JoyAI-VL-Interaction\nL198: \nL199: Responds promptly and keeps guiding as the app screen changes.\nL200: \nL201: #### Doubao\nL202: \nL203: Does not proactively continue the guidance in the recorded comparison.\nL204: \nL205: #### Gemini\nL206: \nL207: Also lacks proactive follow-up in the recorded comparison.\nL208: \nL209: Demo 02\nL210: \nL211: #### Zhuanzhuan App Commentary\nL212: \nL213: App guidance\nL214: \nL215: #### JoyAI-VL-Interaction\nL216: \nL217: Keeps up with the changing phone screen and comments at the requested rhythm.\nL218: \nL219: #### Doubao\nL220: \nL221: Responds only once in this case rather than continuing to follow the app.\nL222: #### Gemini\nL223: \nL224: Shows strong initial recognition but still behaves as a one-shot responder.\nL225: \nL226: Capability 04\nL227: \nL228: ### Live Commentary\nL229: \nL230: Live commentary\nL231: \nL232: Commentary is not just captioning. It requires the model to decide when a scene deserves narration and when silence is better.\nL233: \nL234: Task: Narrate in real time as the scene changes, following specified requirements on tone, cadence, and content.\nL235: \nL236: Demo 01\nL237: \nL238: #### Pet Livestream Commentary\nL239: \nL240: Live commentary\nL241: #### JoyAI-VL-Interaction\nL242: \nL243: Follows the scene and gives grounded commentary as the visible pets change.\nL244: \nL245: #### Doubao\nL246: \nL247: Describes only a few moments among many shots and often misses the actual visual content.\nL248: \nL249: #### Gemini\nL250: \nL251: Gives only one response in this case instead of continuing with the stream.\nL252: \nL253: Demo 02\nL254: \nL255: #### Travel Scene Commentary\nL256: \nL257: Live commentary\nL258: \nL259: #### JoyAI-VL-Interaction\nL260: \nL261: Maintains the requested commentary rhythm and keeps the narration grounded in the changing travel scene.\nL262: #### Doubao\nL263: \nL264: Comments only near the beginning and does not follow the requested repeated timing.\nL265: \nL266: #### Gemini\nL267: \nL268: Produces a mismatched response style and does not sustain live commentary through the scene.\nL269: \nL270: Capability 05\nL271: ### Real-time Counting\nL272: \nL273: Real-time counting\nL274: \nL275: Counting over live video asks the model to maintain state across repeated events, not just recognize a static object.\nL276: \nL277: Task:Count in real time each time a target action or event occurs, such as a dart hitting the board or a burpee in the cases.\nL278: \nL279: Demo 01\nL280: \nL281: #### Dart Throw Counting\nL282: \nL283: Real-time counting\nL284: \nL285: #### JoyAI-VL-Interaction\nL286: \nL287: Catches the right timing for the repeated dart events.\nL288: \nL289: #### Doubao\nL290: \nL291: Replies only twice, with higher delay and less reasonable responses.\nL292: #### Gemini\nL293: \nL294: Says \"let me check\" once and then stops responding in the recorded case.\nL295: \nL296: Demo 02\nL297: \nL298: #### Burpee Counting\nL299: \nL300: Real-time counting\nL301: \nL302: #### JoyAI-VL-Interaction\nL303: \nL304: Tracks repeated exercise actions and updates the count at the right moments.\nL305: \nL306: #### Doubao\nL307: \nL308: Responds only once and misses the continuous counting behavior.\nL309: \nL310: #### Gemini\nL311: \nL312: Does not perform reliable real-time counting in the recorded comparison.\nL313: \nL314: Capability 06\nL315: ### Time Awareness\nL316: \nL317: Time awareness\nL318: \nL319: Some tasks are defined by time itself: answer every few seconds, wait until a timer is over, or report how long an activity has lasted.\nL320: \nL321: Task: Requires the model to gauge elapsed time and respond accordingly, for example replying after 20 seconds, or responding once every three seconds.\nL322: \nL323: Demo 01\nL324: \nL325: #### Timed Cooking Scene\nL326: \nL327: Time awareness\n"
